  "notes": "Shady-camp horror-comic minor, and the harmony is the fingerprint: \"chromatic and half-step-clash motifs\", \"film-score-derived tension harmony rather than soul consonance\". So `halfStepDissonance` is 0.3 — the second highest on this axis after Mobb Deep — over a Phrygian-first scale set. The drums pull the opposite way: Dre-adjacent polish gives 0.92 quantize and 0.53 swing, near-straight, with `velocityTiers` raised to the top of this axis for \"very dry, heavily compressed, Detroit-loud\". The occasional 16th snare ghost is a 0.3 ghost probability on e-and-a positions. ⛔ **i–bII is carried by the Phrygian scale weighting, not by a progression family.** The engine's roman-numeral vocabulary as it exists in `data/` has no attested flat-two token, and inventing `bII` would have been authoring something no generator can read. Phrygian at weight 4 plus `halfStepDissonance` 0.3 produces the half-step clash the research describes without spelling a chord the engine cannot parse. ⚠ The countermelody is not decoration here — the research says \"a plucked bass doubling the motif an octave down\", which is literally `octave_echo` at `registerOffset` -12, so that style carries double weight and `fillGapsOnly` is off. ⚠ `medium` because the research rates it medium-high: the catalog breakdown is first-person, the drum numbers are not. The specific inferred parameter is `session.swing.amount` — \"Dre-adjacent polish\" implies tightness but names no value. ⚠ The i–VII family is INFERRED; the research names i–iv and i–bII. ⚠ The bass ceiling came down from 44 to 43 so it stays under a chord part that starts at 44 — `coherence.rs` requires the highest bass note to sit at or below the lowest chord note, and this model overlapped by 1.",
